Travel SEO fails when a site treats every destination and every month as the same search market. Demand moves with booking windows, traveller type, weather, school holidays and the experiences available in each place. I build the site structure around those differences so the right page is visible while people are still researching.

I work with tour operators, travel agencies, destination organisations, accommodation groups and publishers. The work combines technical SEO with destination planning and useful content. I manage a 44-site portfolio and use the same evidence-led process to decide which pages deserve attention, which pages overlap and which inventory Google should crawl.

## Why Travel SEO Is Different

Travel SEO is shaped by aggregator dominance, seasonal demand and a long path from first search to booking. A traveller may research a place several times before comparing dates, routes, accommodation and activities. The page that introduces the destination is often not the page that receives the booking.

Large OTAs hold strong authority for broad searches. Trying to beat them for every generic destination term wastes budget.

Independent travel brands have a better route through specific combinations of destination, experience, season and audience. A walking holiday in Madeira in February carries clearer intent than a search for European holidays.

Seasonality changes when a page needs to be published. A summer itinerary added in June has missed much of its research window. Search engines need time to crawl the page, understand its place in the site and collect engagement signals before demand peaks.

Location pages create another common problem. Replacing a place name across hundreds of near-identical templates produces thin pages with no reason to rank.

Each useful destination page needs distinct transport details, experiences, timing, local context and a clear route into bookable inventory. International sites also need clean language and country targeting, which is where an [international SEO consultant](/services/international-seo-consultant/) becomes useful.

## What a Travel SEO Consultant Does

A travel SEO consultant connects demand, destinations and live inventory without letting the site become an index of duplicate URLs. The first job is to map what people search for and when they search for it.

**Demand mapping by destination and month.** I group queries by place, experience, traveller and booking period. That map separates early research from comparison and booking intent. It also gives the editorial team a publishing calendar based on when demand starts rather than when the trip takes place.

**Destination and experience architecture.** Country, region, city, route and activity pages need defined roles. I use a [topical map](/services/topical-maps/) to stop a destination guide, tour category and individual itinerary competing for the same query. Internal links then move readers from inspiration to the most relevant commercial page.

**Inventory and faceted URL control.** Dates, durations, departure points, ratings and themes can create thousands of filter combinations. I decide which combinations deserve stable indexable pages and which should remain useful to visitors without entering Google's index.

**Technical checks.** The review covers crawl paths, canonicals, pagination, JavaScript rendering, XML sitemaps, structured data and expired inventory. A [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/) shows where booking systems or filters are wasting crawl budget.

**Reviews and trust signals.** Travel buyers compare risk as well as price. Clear business details, current reviews, transparent inclusions, author experience and accurate destination information help people judge the offer. I structure those signals without inventing proof or forcing generic reassurance onto every page.

## Travel Site Types I Work With

The method changes with the business model. Tour operators need their itineraries and departure pages to support strong destination hubs. Travel agencies need supplier content rewritten around their own audience and expertise rather than copied across the market.

Destination marketing organisations need a clear relationship between broad inspiration, local guides and partner listings. Accommodation groups need properties separated by location and intent while still benefiting from the authority of the group site. Travel publishers need editorial clusters that answer research questions and lead naturally towards a booking or affiliate decision.

I apply 15+ years of technical, content and portfolio SEO to the actual site, its Search Console data and the commercial model behind it. The diagnosis starts with evidence rather than a sector template.

