## Seasonal Content and AI Search

Seasonal travel content should publish three to four months before the peak booking window. That lead time gives destination pages and guides a chance to be crawled, linked and evaluated before search interest reaches its highest point.

The strongest editorial plan works backwards from booking behaviour. Ski content may need to appear before autumn comparison begins.

Christmas market guides need updating before people settle dates. Long-haul itineraries often need an earlier cycle because the decision involves more research and a larger commitment.

AI search adds a second audience for the same work. AI Overviews and ChatGPT tend to use content that presents a clear answer, concrete itinerary, explicit comparison or well-structured fact set.

My portfolio records AI citation data, so recommendations come from pages that are actually being found and cited rather than from a theory about AI visibility. The guide to [optimising content for AI search](/blog/optimise-content-for-ai-search/) explains how answer structure and supporting detail work together.

Useful travel pages still need first-hand accuracy. Update opening times, routes, season dates and entry rules when they change.

Name who checked the information and when. A page shouldn't present an old itinerary as current because an AI system may repeat the error as confidently as a search snippet.

## Travel SEO Questions

**How far ahead of the season should travel content publish?**

Travel content should publish 3 to 4 months before peak booking windows so pages have time to index, earn internal links and gather search signals before demand rises.

**Can a small operator outrank OTAs?**

Not for generic "holidays" terms. A small operator can compete for destination, experience and audience long-tail searches where OTA pages are thin and specialist knowledge gives the page a reason to rank.

**What does travel SEO cost?**

A travel SEO audit costs £495 at a fixed fee. Ongoing retainers start from £1,500 per month and are scoped around the number of destinations, templates and markets involved.

